```sql
SELECT AVG(reading) AS avg_reading, COUNT(reading) AS days_counted
FROM temps;
```

```json
[
  {"avg_reading": 22.0, "days_counted": 3}
]
```

AVG ignores NULLs entirely: it computes 66 / 3 = 22.0, dividing by the number of actual readings. Your 16.5 came from 66 / 4 — dividing by all rows including the missing day, which treats "no reading" as "a reading of zero" without saying so. If a missing day genuinely should count as zero for your purpose, say it explicitly with AVG(COALESCE(reading, 0)); otherwise trust AVG's skip-NULL behavior, and use COUNT(reading) alongside so the denominator is visible in the output.

```sql
-- SQLite
SELECT name FROM people WHERE name LIKE 'ana';
-- Postgres, case-sensitive
SELECT name FROM people WHERE name LIKE 'ana';
-- Postgres, case-insensitive
SELECT name FROM people WHERE name ILIKE 'ana';
```

```json
[
  {"engine": "SQLite LIKE", "matches": ["Ana", "ana", "ANA"], "count": 3},
  {"engine": "Postgres LIKE", "matches": ["ana"], "count": 1},
  {"engine": "Postgres ILIKE", "matches": ["Ana", "ana", "ANA"], "count": 3}
]
```

LIKE's case behavior is not standardized — it is an engine (and even collation) property. SQLite's LIKE is case-insensitive for ASCII letters by default, so all three spellings match. Postgres's LIKE is case-sensitive, matching only the exact lowercase 'ana', and Postgres offers ILIKE as the explicit case-insensitive variant. MySQL usually behaves case-insensitively because its default collations are. The practical lesson: a search feature tested on one engine can silently change behavior when ported. If case-insensitivity is a requirement, write it deliberately — ILIKE where it exists, or LOWER(name) LIKE 'ana' portably — instead of inheriting whatever the local default happens to be.

```sql
SELECT v FROM t1 UNION SELECT v FROM t2;
SELECT v FROM t1 UNION ALL SELECT v FROM t2;
```

```json
[
  {"query": "UNION", "rows": [1, 2, 3], "row_count": 3},
  {"query": "UNION ALL", "rows": [1, 2, 2, 3], "row_count": 4}
]
```

UNION concatenates the two result sets and then removes duplicate rows — the value 2, present in both tables, survives only once, so 3 rows come back (in no guaranteed order). UNION ALL is pure concatenation: every row from both sides, duplicates included, 4 rows. The consequences: UNION pays a deduplication cost (a sort or hash over the whole combined set), and it silently hides genuine duplicates — if t1 legitimately contained two rows of 2, UNION would still show one. Default to UNION ALL unless duplicates are actually a problem you intend to remove; it is faster and it does not editorialize your data.

